WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:01.710
Instructor: When you're working on a project

00:01.710 --> 00:03.000
and you're using Git,

00:03.000 --> 00:04.680
there are often situations

00:04.680 --> 00:07.140
where you have files, directories

00:07.140 --> 00:09.630
that you just don't want Git to know about

00:09.630 --> 00:11.520
or to track at any point.

00:11.520 --> 00:13.890
You want them to be completely ignored

00:13.890 --> 00:15.810
and there's a mechanism for doing this.

00:15.810 --> 00:18.633
We can create a particular file called .gitignore,

00:19.890 --> 00:21.420
but before we go into that,

00:21.420 --> 00:23.760
let's talk about why you might want this.

00:23.760 --> 00:25.560
One of the most common situations

00:25.560 --> 00:27.090
is if you're working on an application,

00:27.090 --> 00:30.270
you're writing code where you're using API keys,

00:30.270 --> 00:33.810
credentials, any sort of application secret

00:33.810 --> 00:37.590
that is tied potentially to a credit card like from AWS

00:37.590 --> 00:40.320
or some API that you're using

00:40.320 --> 00:43.470
where you don't want those to get out into the world.

00:43.470 --> 00:45.750
So you put them in a separate file

00:45.750 --> 00:48.000
and if you push your code up to GitHub,

00:48.000 --> 00:49.830
which I realize we haven't talked about,

00:49.830 --> 00:52.380
but if you share that code and you make it public,

00:52.380 --> 00:54.810
you don't want that file to be included.

00:54.810 --> 00:57.150
So you'll still have the file yourself locally

00:57.150 --> 00:59.280
but you don't want Git to track it.

00:59.280 --> 01:02.280
Other things include operating system files.

01:02.280 --> 01:05.853
So on a Mac there are these pesky .DS_Store files

01:05.853 --> 01:09.390
that have nothing to do with your project or your code.

01:09.390 --> 01:10.980
They just show up in directories.

01:10.980 --> 01:12.870
They're system hidden files,

01:12.870 --> 01:14.820
so you don't want to track those with Git.

01:14.820 --> 01:18.420
There are similar files on Windows with a different name.

01:18.420 --> 01:19.980
Other things, log files

01:19.980 --> 01:23.040
or any files that are output by your application

01:23.040 --> 01:25.770
that could easily be generated again.

01:25.770 --> 01:28.230
So if you have an application when you run it,

01:28.230 --> 01:32.280
it generates some, you know, reports every single day

01:32.280 --> 01:34.860
and you regenerate that report on demand,

01:34.860 --> 01:36.150
you don't really don't need Git

01:36.150 --> 01:38.190
to track that and save that report

01:38.190 --> 01:41.310
because you can regenerate that report, if that makes sense.

01:41.310 --> 01:42.143
And then lastly,

01:42.143 --> 01:45.660
a really common scenario is dependencies or packages.

01:45.660 --> 01:47.820
So if you've ever worked with Node or Python

01:47.820 --> 01:50.400
or really any well any language

01:50.400 --> 01:53.220
and you work with a bunch of dependencies that you download

01:53.220 --> 01:56.880
and install, those do not need to be tracked by Git.

01:56.880 --> 01:57.900
That could be hundreds

01:57.900 --> 01:59.670
or maybe thousands of different folders

01:59.670 --> 02:02.910
and files that you can easily go and get at any point.

02:02.910 --> 02:06.600
You don't need to bother tracking the dependencies themself.

02:06.600 --> 02:08.940
So I'm gonna show you an example of how we do this.

02:08.940 --> 02:12.510
As I mentioned, the mechanism is through this file

02:12.510 --> 02:14.010
with a very particular name.

02:14.010 --> 02:16.690
It's a hidden file that we create .gitignore

02:17.856 --> 02:21.210
and if we put that really anywhere in a repository

02:21.210 --> 02:23.160
but traditionally we'll put it

02:23.160 --> 02:26.100
in the root of the repository, the top of it,

02:26.100 --> 02:27.686
although it doesn't have to be there,

02:27.686 --> 02:30.360
we create a file with that name.

02:30.360 --> 02:33.990
Git will look for that file and inside of it

02:33.990 --> 02:36.480
we can tell Git what to ignore.

02:36.480 --> 02:38.700
So we can put exact file names.

02:38.700 --> 02:42.630
So the first bullet point here is .DS_Store,

02:42.630 --> 02:45.210
Git will ignore a file with that name.

02:45.210 --> 02:48.899
We can give it folder names by putting a slash at the end.

02:48.899 --> 02:51.600
And we can also do fancier patterns.

02:51.600 --> 02:56.130
For example, this line here, *.log,

02:56.130 --> 02:59.430
this is a wild card that will match anything.

02:59.430 --> 03:01.560
I think anything except for a slash.

03:01.560 --> 03:03.060
It will match any character

03:03.060 --> 03:05.760
followed by the extension .log.

03:05.760 --> 03:09.630
So this allows you to tell Git, ignore all log files.

03:09.630 --> 03:11.040
I don't want to have to write them out.

03:11.040 --> 03:12.570
I don't know what their names will be.

03:12.570 --> 03:14.610
Just anything with .log.

03:14.610 --> 03:16.770
So I'll just quickly show you some examples.

03:16.770 --> 03:18.990
Again, we haven't really gone into GitHub at all

03:18.990 --> 03:21.030
but on GitHub there are projects

03:21.030 --> 03:23.790
and pretty much every single project here

03:23.790 --> 03:26.910
is going to have a .gitignore file.

03:26.910 --> 03:31.410
And inside we see some of what I talked about .DS_Store.

03:31.410 --> 03:35.310
We see directories like the reports directory, node modules,

03:35.310 --> 03:36.570
if you ever work with Node,

03:36.570 --> 03:38.790
that's where all the packages are installed.

03:38.790 --> 03:40.890
So we're ignoring that.

03:40.890 --> 03:42.240
Here's another example.

03:42.240 --> 03:45.286
The app signal, if you're familiar with that,

03:45.286 --> 03:47.730
.gitignore, there it is.

03:47.730 --> 03:49.290
And there's stuff in here,

03:49.290 --> 03:51.630
particular files and folder names.

03:51.630 --> 03:55.350
They're different because this is an Android application

03:55.350 --> 03:58.020
versus Faker I was showing you as a JavaScript app.

03:58.020 --> 03:59.070
Here's TensorFlow,

03:59.070 --> 04:01.268
another really popular open source project

04:01.268 --> 04:05.130
instead of here .gitignore, right?

04:05.130 --> 04:06.900
And then we see similar stuff

04:06.900 --> 04:08.940
but now some Python specific files

04:08.940 --> 04:12.450
and folders that they're telling Git to always ignore.

04:12.450 --> 04:14.197
So I'm gonna demonstrate this

04:14.197 --> 04:18.030
and to do this I'm actually gonna make a new repository.

04:18.030 --> 04:21.150
It doesn't really make sense to ignore things in my novel.

04:21.150 --> 04:23.610
Maybe it does, but I'm just gonna make a new demo

04:23.610 --> 04:26.340
and make a simple node application.

04:26.340 --> 04:28.620
So I'm not in a Git repository now,

04:28.620 --> 04:30.600
I'm gonna make a directory.

04:30.600 --> 04:33.030
I'll just call this GitIgnoreDemo.

04:33.030 --> 04:35.100
I'll cd in there.

04:35.100 --> 04:37.110
I'll type git status.

04:37.110 --> 04:37.943
Okay, good.

04:37.943 --> 04:41.640
It's not one yet, but now I'm going to initialize get init,

04:41.640 --> 04:44.880
and I'm going to run npm init

04:44.880 --> 04:47.673
so that I can use some Node packages in here.

04:48.540 --> 04:49.950
So if you're not familiar with Node,

04:49.950 --> 04:51.712
it really doesn't matter at this point.

04:51.712 --> 04:54.210
I'm just gonna install some stuff

04:54.210 --> 04:55.680
and I'm going to ignore it.

04:55.680 --> 04:57.510
But you can ignore any other files

04:57.510 --> 04:59.730
using the same stuff that I'm gonna show you.

04:59.730 --> 05:01.290
So I'm gonna make a file.

05:01.290 --> 05:03.930
I'll pretend you know to write a basic JavaScript app

05:03.930 --> 05:08.930
in here, index.js, maybe I'll make a secrets file as well.

05:09.150 --> 05:12.840
Secrets. I'll make it a text file

05:12.840 --> 05:15.300
and then I open it up in my text editor.

05:15.300 --> 05:17.100
Let's put some stuff in here.

05:17.100 --> 05:18.980
So my index.js,

05:18.980 --> 05:22.170
I'm just gonna write some very basic JavaScript code

05:22.170 --> 05:26.760
like console.log, hello world.

05:26.760 --> 05:29.520
All right, so whatever, this is my application code.

05:29.520 --> 05:34.520
Then I'll have some secret like API_Key equals

05:35.790 --> 05:37.770
let's just say that's some secret key

05:37.770 --> 05:39.870
I don't want anyone to know about,

05:39.870 --> 05:41.640
but I need it for my app.

05:41.640 --> 05:45.513
And then let's go ahead and make our first commit.

05:46.530 --> 05:48.390
So it would be nice

05:48.390 --> 05:51.480
if I could make a commit and just do git add dot

05:51.480 --> 05:55.920
but I can't because that will include secrets.txt.

05:55.920 --> 05:58.720
So I need to add the index first

05:59.820 --> 06:02.070
and then I'll also add my package.json

06:02.070 --> 06:05.160
which is a generated file from Node.

06:05.160 --> 06:09.243
And then I'll commit, you know, start project or something.

06:10.260 --> 06:11.640
It doesn't matter what.

06:11.640 --> 06:12.600
Okay.

06:12.600 --> 06:15.870
And of course we still have this file that's untracked

06:15.870 --> 06:17.160
and we want it to stay that way.

06:17.160 --> 06:18.930
We don't want it to be tracked.

06:18.930 --> 06:21.330
So if I do some more work in here,

06:21.330 --> 06:25.590
I'm just gonna copy that a couple times.

06:25.590 --> 06:27.749
We'll pretend I just wrote a lot of code,

06:27.749 --> 06:30.060
then I'm gonna head over here again

06:30.060 --> 06:34.830
to my terminal and I need to add that change

06:34.830 --> 06:36.570
but I can't do git add dot,

06:36.570 --> 06:39.283
I need to add just the index.js, right?

06:39.283 --> 06:43.680
And then I can commit again, you know, add new feature.

06:43.680 --> 06:47.220
This is a horrible commit message, but pretend it's not.

06:47.220 --> 06:48.053
All right.

06:48.053 --> 06:51.330
So now I'm gonna go and install something with NPM.

06:51.330 --> 06:53.703
I'll install React.

06:55.110 --> 06:56.700
I'm using NPM in here.

06:56.700 --> 07:00.480
And we can see I have a new directory, node_modules,

07:00.480 --> 07:04.530
and it has react in there and some of react's dependencies.

07:04.530 --> 07:07.260
So if I type git status now,

07:07.260 --> 07:08.220
it also notices

07:08.220 --> 07:13.220
that node_modules now exist and my secrets file still there.

07:13.230 --> 07:15.695
There's this thing called package-lock.json.

07:15.695 --> 07:19.110
The package.json is modified, which I'm cool with.

07:19.110 --> 07:20.490
I want to track that.

07:20.490 --> 07:22.950
That's where the dependencies are listed.

07:22.950 --> 07:25.080
So it tells anybody who gets this code,

07:25.080 --> 07:26.880
I need react, but we don't actually need

07:26.880 --> 07:29.250
to track the folder that includes React.

07:29.250 --> 07:31.740
We can always go get that whenever necessary.

07:31.740 --> 07:33.990
So now let's add in our .gitignore file

07:33.990 --> 07:36.420
so that this will be ignored.

07:36.420 --> 07:38.550
I'm going to make a new file.

07:38.550 --> 07:40.241
I can do it from here.

07:40.241 --> 07:41.250
I'll just do it

07:41.250 --> 07:43.290
from the command line I guess to be consistent.

07:43.290 --> 07:44.733
Touch .gitignore,

07:46.440 --> 07:48.300
and as I mentioned, it's conventional to put it

07:48.300 --> 07:51.060
in the root of your project directory.

07:51.060 --> 07:53.574
So now I have that file and then in here

07:53.574 --> 07:55.812
I can write certain files

07:55.812 --> 07:58.020
and patterns that I want to be ignored.

07:58.020 --> 07:59.013
So for example,

07:59.940 --> 08:02.580
I don't know if there's a .DS_Store file in here,

08:02.580 --> 08:03.930
no, there's not at the moment

08:03.930 --> 08:07.020
but I don't know why it pops up, sometimes it just does.

08:07.020 --> 08:09.120
So I'm saying ignore that.

08:09.120 --> 08:13.893
Please also ignore the exact file secrets.txt.

08:14.970 --> 08:19.560
That's the name of the file and the node_modules directory.

08:19.560 --> 08:22.740
So I need a slash, if it's just node_modules like this,

08:22.740 --> 08:23.910
it thinks it's a file.

08:23.910 --> 08:27.300
This tells Git it's a directory, node_modules.

08:27.300 --> 08:31.007
So over here, if I type git status now, whoa, look at that.

08:31.007 --> 08:32.396
Very different.

08:32.396 --> 08:35.730
Now we no longer see well, hmm,

08:35.730 --> 08:37.890
I got rid of it unfortunately, I cleared.

08:37.890 --> 08:41.430
But earlier it was tracking node_modules and secrets.

08:41.430 --> 08:44.490
Now we just see .gitignore

08:44.490 --> 08:47.340
because we did change that file and it exists.

08:47.340 --> 08:49.334
We want to track the .gitignore file.

08:49.334 --> 08:52.317
Then also this package-lock.json

08:52.317 --> 08:54.750
and that's fine to track with Git.

08:54.750 --> 08:59.750
So now I can just do things like git add dot if I wanted to

08:59.883 --> 09:03.570
and I can commit and then I can make some commit message.

09:03.570 --> 09:04.920
In this case, doesn't really matter

09:04.920 --> 09:05.820
I haven't done any work.

09:05.820 --> 09:08.463
But I'll just say, you know, add .gitignore file.

09:10.260 --> 09:12.810
And now anytime I make changes in those files,

09:12.810 --> 09:15.900
if I change my secrets file right there

09:15.900 --> 09:17.520
I'll do something else in here.

09:17.520 --> 09:19.609
Let's just add onto that key.

09:19.609 --> 09:24.450
I save, normally Git would notice, but now it says nothing.

09:24.450 --> 09:26.970
It's ignoring this file entirely.

09:26.970 --> 09:29.340
So there's a lot to write in .gitignore,

09:29.340 --> 09:31.650
especially for complicated projects.

09:31.650 --> 09:33.420
You can have all sorts of stuff in there.

09:33.420 --> 09:36.600
And to help out, there are tools online,

09:36.600 --> 09:37.590
show you one quickly.

09:37.590 --> 09:40.080
This is on the GitHub documentation.

09:40.080 --> 09:42.990
They recommend this one site .gitignore.io

09:42.990 --> 09:45.330
where you can specify what you're working in.

09:45.330 --> 09:47.680
For example, if you're working on a Python app,

09:50.370 --> 09:53.730
this is a recommended starting place for .gitignore.

09:53.730 --> 09:57.120
And you may not have files with all of these patterns

09:57.120 --> 09:59.370
or these extensions or these directory names

09:59.370 --> 10:02.010
but they're common enough that you can just include it.

10:02.010 --> 10:03.030
It's really not adding

10:03.030 --> 10:05.220
a lot of overhead to your application

10:05.220 --> 10:07.980
and they have similar .gitignore templates for JavaScript

10:07.980 --> 10:10.110
and pretty much any other thing you can think of.

10:10.110 --> 10:12.019
So that's an intro to .gitignore files.

10:12.019 --> 10:14.340
There's a lot to them, right?

10:14.340 --> 10:15.510
The patterns you can write,

10:15.510 --> 10:18.810
you can actually go on the Git Docs and read about it.

10:18.810 --> 10:20.460
There's more that we didn't even go into.

10:20.460 --> 10:24.750
For example, two stars is different from one star.

10:24.750 --> 10:27.660
A question mark is a way to match a single character.

10:27.660 --> 10:30.150
If you're familiar with regular expressions notation,

10:30.150 --> 10:31.620
it's kind of similar here.

10:31.620 --> 10:34.170
But anyway, most of the time it's good enough

10:34.170 --> 10:36.690
to just be able to ignore file names, directories,

10:36.690 --> 10:39.810
and file extensions using the wild card.

10:39.810 --> 10:41.570
Okay, so that's .gitignore.
